description:
In smime_tests.rs, three test structs hold private key bytes as struct fields with no zeroize-on-drop:

  struct TestRsaDecryptionKey { private_key: RsaPrivateKey, cert: Certificate }
  struct TestRsaSigningKey    { private_key: rsa::pkcs1v15::SigningKey<Sha256>, cert: Certificate }
  struct TestEcP256DecryptionKey { secret_key: p256::SecretKey, cert: Certificate }
  struct TestEcP256SigningKey    { signing_key: p256::ecdsa::SigningKey, cert: Certificate }

None of these derive or implement Zeroize / ZeroizeOnDrop.

What goes wrong: in test binaries (which may run in CI environments, containers, or shared hosts), private key bytes that are identical to the hardcoded PKCS#8 hex constants in the test file remain in process memory after the struct drops. A memory-dump tool or a use-after-free in a different allocation can read the key bytes. While these are test fixtures, not production keys, the pattern demonstrates that the crate does not enforce key-lifecycle hygiene at the consumer layer, and a copy-paste of the test struct pattern into production code would be insecure.

The underlying RsaPrivateKey and p256::SecretKey types do implement ZeroizeOnDrop, but only if held directly — wrapping them in a non-Zeroize struct defeats that guarantee if the struct is moved or the fields are copied out.

Fix: add #[derive(Zeroize, ZeroizeOnDrop)] to all four test structs, or at least add a #[zeroize(drop)] annotation to the key fields.
